//! S6, STEP (a): A SHADOW `@relation` OVER `Decl`, inserted beside the real table,
//! read by nobody yet. `DeclRow` mirrors `Decl`'s own fields (decls.av) so a parity
//! keeper can prove the derive carries the real shape before anything reads through
//! it. `id`/`file`/`stmt`/`parent`/`root`/`lo`/`hi` are process-local (`@dense`,
//! `@local`): a dense id, an offset or an arena index means nothing across processes,
//! so none of them enters the stable hash. `file` keeps an `@index` so a reader can
//! ask for a file's rows, the shape the grain law (S1) records against.
//!
//! RECORDED TRIGGER: `@key (file, owner, name, sibling)` arrives when `File` is a
//! relation and `file` stops being `@local` (decision 10(a)) — owner: ERRORS. That
//! tuple, not the interning string, is the row's stable name (townhall doc §6,
//! "same-(owner, name) siblings only"): the key and the name a witness stores a
//! dependency under are one derivation, or a second caller gets a wrong answer. No
//! `sibling` column exists on `Decl` yet — it is an ordinal among same-(owner, name)
//! declarations, computed, not stored, and the shadow carries none until it does.
//! `DeclRow` is keyless meanwhile: a rerun replaces the whole set, which is sound for
//! a shadow inserted by one query (`mint`) and read by nobody.
use core.{DeclId, FileId, StmtId, ExprId, DeclKind}
use @std.relation.{relation}

@relation
export type DeclRow = {
    id: DeclId,
    @local file: FileId,
    @local stmt: StmtId,
    @index name: string,
    kind: DeclKind,
    exported: bool,
    owner: string,
    @local parent: DeclId?,
    nested: bool,
    @local root: ExprId?,
    @local lo: int,
    @local hi: int,
}
